5 Remove Old Kernels 

 

  If you are running a Linux system, old kernels can take up significant space. You can remove them using: 

 

 @ For Debian/Ubuntu: 

   

 > apt-get autoremove --purge 

 

 @ For Red Hat/CentOS: 

   

 > sudo package-cleanup --oldkernels --count=2 

 

6  Manage Log Files : Truncate Large Log Files: If you have large log files, you can truncate them instead of deleting them: 

 

 > truncate -s 0 /var/log/<your-log-file.log> 

 

7 Clear System Cache: 

   @ You can clear the system cache using the following command: 

 

 > sync; echo 3 | sudo tee /proc/sys/vm/drop_caches 

   

  @ Clear User Cache: 

 

 > su - username  

 > rm -rf ~/.cache/*   

 > rm -rf /home/username/.cache/*  this command is used to clear the cache without switching into any user while sitting as a root user. 

 

 > rm -rf ~/.cache/mozilla/firefox/*  this command is used to clear the cache of any web browser. 

 > rm -rf ~/.cache/google-chrome/*  this command is used to clear the cache of chrome browser. 

 

8  Identify Problematic Processes: 

 

  @ Look for processes in a 'D' state (uninterruptible sleep) using this command to find processes that may be causing high load. 

 

      > ps aux | awk '$8 ~ /D/ { print $0 } 

 

  @ (R), indicating they are actively using CPU resources. 

 

      > ps aux | awk '$8 ~ /R/ { print $0 } 

    

  @ (S), meaning they are waiting for an event (like I/O) to occur but can be woken up. 

 

      > ps aux | awk '$8 ~ /S/ { print $0 }  

 

   @ (D), indicating they are waiting for I/O operations to complete and cannot be interrupted.     

 

      > ps aux | awk '$8 ~ /D/ { print $0 }  

 

   @ (T), meaning they have been stopped, usually by receiving a signal. 

    

      > ps aux | awk '$8 ~ /T/ { print $0 }  

 

   @ (t), indicating they are being traced by another process (often used for debugging). 

 

      > ps aux | awk '$8 ~ /t/ { print $0 }  

 

    @ (Z), meaning they have completed execution but still have an entry in the process table, waiting for their parent process to read their exit status. 

       

      > ps aux | awk '$8 ~ /Z/ { print $0 }  

 

